BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Maurer School of Law
DTSTART:20261106T140000Z
DTEND:20261106T211500Z
LOCATION:LOWELL E. BAIER HALL/IU MAURER SCHOOL OF LAW
SUMMARY:ILJ Symposium: Evolving Frontiers in Criminal Law: Pregnancy\, Fa
 mily\, and Identity
DESCRIPTION:The Symposium originated from the book\, Pink Crime: Fighting
  Against the Criminalization of Motherhood\, Pregnancy\, and Queer Identi
 ty\, authored by Professor Valena Beety\, the Robert H. McKinney Professo
 r of Law at Indiana University Maurer School of Law and a founding board 
 member of the Indiana Innocence Project. Pink Crime focuses on the ways t
 hat criminal law is strategically used to limit the bodily autonomy of wo
 men and queer people\, combined with a discussion of how the system as a 
 whole targets the most vulnerable populations while allowing bias\, stigm
 a\, and unreliable evidence to influence prosecution decisions\, especial
 ly in situations where no crime occurred at all. As the book explores\, t
 he modern American legal system disproportionately punishes and often cri
 minalizes identity\, specifically in situations where pregnancy\, motherh
 ood\, or queer identity are present.  &#160\;  This Symposium worked to e
 xpand upon Professor Beety's work in Pink Crime by bringing in additional
  perspectives to illustrate how existing and developing legal mechanisms 
 strip away basic rights and lay the groundwork for more oppressive measur
 es in the future. The Symposium convened a group of nationally-respected 
 experts in wrongful convictions\, criminal law\, reproductive justice\, f
 amily law\, family policing\, faulty forensics\, gender law\, and health 
 law\, among others. Their combined collection of work addressed emerging 
 trends in prosecution\, evidence\, and punishment related to the topic.  
 &#160\;  This area of law is evolving too quickly for large portions of t
 he legal academy to keep up. Importantly\, this Symposium fills a gap in 
 the existing legal scholarship. Discussions on pregnancy crime and gender
  affirming care are reaching the highest levels of state\, national\, and
  international governance\, and informed scholarship is paramount as deci
 sions continue to be made that affect massive amounts of individuals acro
 ss the country\, and even across the globe. Therefore\, this Symposium ai
 ms to analyze the legal\, social\, and political dimensions of identity c
 riminalization and provide timely theory\, analysis\, and practical solut
 ions for professionals to draw on as we move forward.ILJ associates are r
 equired to attend at least two panels. Attendance will be taken.Schedule&
 #160\;9:00 – 10:00 AM Panel 1: The Prosecution of Pregnancy Behaviors a
 nd Outcomes10:15 – 11:15 AM Panel 2: Criminal Law After Dobbs11:30 AM �
 �� 12:30 PM Panel 3: Family Regulation Systems and Criminal Law2:00 – 3
 :00 PM Panel 4: Criminalizing Queer Life and Family3:15 – 4:15 PM Panel
  5: Systemic Failures along the Path to Wrongful Convictions
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  The Symposium originated from the book\
 , <em>Pink Crime: Fighting Against the Criminalization of Motherhood\, Pr
 egnancy\, and Queer Identity</em>\, authored by Professor Valena Beety\, 
 the Robert H. McKinney Professor of Law at Indiana University Maurer Scho
 ol of Law and a founding board member of the Indiana Innocence Project. <
 em>Pink Crime</em> focuses on the ways that criminal law is strategically
  used to limit the bodily autonomy of women and queer people\, combined w
 ith a discussion of how the system as a whole targets the most vulnerable
  populations while allowing bias\, stigma\, and unreliable evidence to in
 fluence prosecution decisions\, especially in situations where no crime o
 ccurred at all. As the book explores\, the modern American legal system d
 isproportionately punishes and often criminalizes identity\, specifically
  in situations where pregnancy\, motherhood\, or queer identity are prese
 nt.</p><p>  &#160\;</p><p>  This Symposium worked to expand upon Professo
 r Beety's work in <em>Pink Crime</em> by bringing in additional perspecti
 ves to illustrate how existing and developing legal mechanisms strip away
  basic rights and lay the groundwork for more oppressive measures in the 
 future. The Symposium convened a group of nationally-respected experts in
  wrongful convictions\, criminal law\, reproductive justice\, family law\
 , family policing\, faulty forensics\, gender law\, and health law\, amon
 g others. Their combined collection of work addressed emerging trends in 
 prosecution\, evidence\, and punishment related to the topic.</p><p>  &#1
 60\;</p><p>  This area of law is evolving too quickly for large portions 
 of the legal academy to keep up. Importantly\, this Symposium fills a gap
  in the existing legal scholarship. Discussions on pregnancy crime and ge
 nder affirming care are reaching the highest levels of state\, national\,
  and international governance\, and informed scholarship is paramount as 
 decisions continue to be made that affect massive amounts of individuals 
 across the country\, and even across the globe. Therefore\, this Symposiu
 m aims to analyze the legal\, social\, and political dimensions of identi
 ty criminalization and provide timely theory\, analysis\, and practical s
 olutions for professionals to draw on as we move forward.</p><p><span sty
 le="text-decoration: underline\;">ILJ associates are required to attend a
 t least two panels. Attendance will be taken.</span></p><p><strong>Schedu
 le&#160\;</strong></p><p><strong>9:00 – 10:00 AM Panel 1: <span style="
 text-decoration: underline\;">The Prosecution of Pregnancy Behaviors and 
 Outcomes</span></strong></p><p><strong>10:15 – 11:15 AM Panel 2: <span 
 style="text-decoration: underline\;">Criminal Law After Dobbs</span></str
 ong></p><p><strong>11:30 AM – 12:30 PM Panel 3: <span style="text-decor
 ation: underline\;">Family Regulation Systems and Criminal Law</span></st
 rong></p><p><strong>2:00 – 3:00 PM Panel 4: <span style="text-decoratio
 n: underline\;">Criminalizing Queer Life and Family</span></strong></p><p
 ><strong>3:15 – 4:15 PM Panel 5: <span style="text-decoration: underlin
 e\;">Systemic Failures along the Path to Wrongful Convictions</span></str
 ong></p>
